h institutions  English literature and I must confess  probed very well into that particular  inquiry of various forms of English  lager and ale and so it came to pass  that many years ago I was introduced to  that British of all British institutions  the pub when I went to the pub with my  friends about seven or eight of us  sitting in a semi-circle we proceeded to  order everyone ordered their own kind of  drink and the waitress without writing  down a single note took note of our  orders proceeded to the bar and came  back with each order and placed it in  front of each person who had ordered it  I was quite taken by the professionalism  of this woman and I remarked on it I  said ma'am you're a real pro and all of  my English friends grew very quiet was  that awkward kind of silence where I  knew I had trod upon something on nice  but was not quite sure what it was so I  turned to my friend next to me I said  what did I just do he said oh you've  just called the young lady a prostitute  and so it comes to pass that George were  not sure observed that American England  are two nations  separated by the same language and now  to our discussion of Babel in our  contemporary use there are a plethora of  words a plethora of words which have  developed new meanings and yet  pronounced the same way as they've  always been pronounced they are employed  in very different ways and for very  different specific political purposes  the topic proposed to me by our host  this evening was the meaning of social  justice how to think about social  justice and this to be truthful is only  one of a veritable litany of words  consider the way in which this list may  fall upon your ears progressive  social market social gospel equality gay  marriage marriage equality fairness  sustainability differently-abled  cooperation investment social commitment  the common good liberal or liberalism  pluralism tolerance diversity and the  list can go on it is enough to drive a  linguist to despair is it not and I must  confess to you that I want my words back  and among the words I want back perhaps  chief of all is the word Madonna  now let us proceed to sort through this  mess and see if we can come to some  sensible comprehension of what we mean  when we talk about social justice and  I'm aware that it is rooted  fundamentally in justice itself now I'm  also aware that there are many  commentators not all of whom are enemies  of sensibility and freedom and the human  person who want to just give up on the  words or the phrase social justice I am  not prepared to abandon it altogether  and I hope to make my case to you now  simply put what we mean by justice is to  say that human beings are entitled to be  treated in accord with what they are due  treatment in a court with dessert you  could put it on a bumper sticker  I suggest that some of the phrases of my  previous litany are really not sensible  concepts but a thing called a plan ISM  that is employing more words than needed  to express oneself a kind of redundancy  now this is not necessarily a bad thing  it can be a benign thing just something  we do for emphasis we talk about tuna  fish do we not or free gift or a true  fact it may be the use of a term for the  sake of emphasis to augment or  underscore something that's important in  the Italian language this occurs for  example when you say I love you  literally to translate I love you into  Italian is to say te amo te amo  now that's the two words are mo being  the first person singular of the  infinitive amore amore to love yo amo te  amo but most often when you say I love  you in Italian other than a kind of  idiomatic phrase which doesn't pertain  here you would say yo te amo well you've  already said I in the first person our  mo but you would say I I love you in  order to emphasis that it is me who  loves you to speak as some do of a  social market is to employ this player  in ISM after all how could a market be  anything other than social