eful that this need not be  the case that organizations like  Hillsdale College and the Acton  Institute and the plethora of other  organizations and movements and  encounters in your homes will ensure  that those roots are nourished and  rediscovered and re articulated so that  we can build an army of people who will  go to the barricades to defend a society  worthy of the human person the free and  the virtuous of society thank you very  much for your attention  thank you  Bellino we now have some time for  questions if you have a question please  wait until the microphone is brought to  you so we all may hear the question  thank you  father Scirocco thank you for your  leadership in the Acton Institute the  Neil Cavuto show in May when your book  came out you made a statement that I  thought was very interesting you said  that it was through the study of  economics that you came back to faith  yes I'd be curious and I'm sure other  people here would be too if you could  explain that thank you  it is curious isn't it of course the the  the the short answer to that question is  to read the book well I would be  negligent as a capitalist not to put a  commercial in for the book really a lot  of what I've said is really that I was  involved with the political left for a  number of years in the early 1970s I was  you name the movement and I was part of  it I was part of the early feminist  movement the early-game movement the  farmworkers movement the boycott of  Gallo wine which I still tried to  boycott when I can I said that once and  found out that the an executive from  Gallo wine was in the audience  we haven't gotten any donations from  them heavily Christian and various other  other movements and a person introduced  me to a friend of his a neighbor of his  and he gave me some books books you'll  know Friedrich Hayek Friedrich Basquiat  Milton Friedman and others I'm sure that  Basquiat spoke at Hillsdale College  right at some point and these these  books have got me thinking about  questions of private property not not  God questions of private property and  the more I probed the question of who is  it that has the right to private  property kind of in the way that I've  done here tonight the more I thought  about human transcendence that's why I'm  so passionate about  economics from an anthropological point  of view because as I looked at that and  saw the human transcendence it was there  that I kind of bumped back into God it's  not a formal proof for the existence of  God I we could talk about that but for  me it was a reencounter it just made  everything fit together it just all hung  together for me and made great sense but  it was of great concern to me that as I  returned to the faith that I had  abandoned as a child the Catholic faith  I came back to a church that had been  turned upside down while I had been gone  and so that there were a lot of the  kinds of people that I was demonstrating  with on the streets of Los Angeles in  the early 1970s now in my seminary in  the 1980s and it was then that I  determined that I wanted to do something  about that first by writing and then by  founding the Acton Institute with Chris  Maron who is here tonight  some 22 years ago  father sir echo I've heard your name and  written your read your works and it's a  real pleasure to hear this evening thank  you I've understand that justice is the  delivery of what one is due who  determines what what is due and does  that work individually or does it work  for groups of people those are very good  questions and in fact I alluded to those  answers here though in a very cryptic  form who determines what one is do I  think there are two dimensions to two  levels to answer that the first is that  the natural law tells us who human  beings are and thus what human being  beings are do by virtue of our intellect  by virtue of our need to be able to  support ourselves our creativity we are  we have of necessity freedom in order to  